WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.999
Opening a file is a security-sensitive decision because the open operation chooses what the program is allowed to do.

2
00:00:06.999 --> 00:00:11.199
Read, write, append, create, truncate, and exclusive-create modes have different consequences.

3
00:00:11.199 --> 00:00:15.398
A safe design uses the narrowest mode that fits the intended operation.

4
00:00:15.398 --> 00:00:18.548
Reading a configuration file should not require write access.

5
00:00:18.548 --> 00:00:22.047
Appending to a log should not accidentally truncate existing data.

6
00:00:22.047 --> 00:00:27.297
Creating a new output should not silently overwrite a file that should have been preserved.

7
00:00:27.297 --> 00:00:30.446
Permissions and access rights matter at the same boundary.

8
00:00:30.446 --> 00:00:35.696
Sensitive output should not be created with broad permissions just because the default is convenient.

9
00:00:35.696 --> 00:00:40.245
A service account should not need access to directories it never legitimately uses.

10
00:00:40.245 --> 00:00:44.095
Least privilege keeps file mistakes smaller and makes review more concrete.

11
00:00:44.095 --> 00:00:50.044
Existence checks followed by later opens can be fragile if another process can modify the target directory.

12
00:00:50.044 --> 00:00:57.393
The defensive idea is not to memorize every platform flag in this course, but to recognize the check-then-use assumption.

13
00:00:57.393 --> 00:01:05.792
If correctness depends on the file not changing between operations, the code should use an API or workflow that enforces that assumption as closely

14
00:01:05.792 --> 00:01:07.192
as the platform allows.

15
00:01:07.192 --> 00:01:08.942
Always handle open failure deliberately.

16
00:01:08.942 --> 00:01:16.641
Failure may be normal: a file is missing, permissions are narrow, a directory is unavailable, or a path policy rejected the request.

17
00:01:16.641 --> 00:01:21.540
Good code reports enough context for diagnosis without leaking sensitive paths or internal details.

18
00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.040
It also documents who may modify the location being opened.
